From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stephan-xyz <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 15:33:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] memory: out of memory in fork, mremap and DMA allocation is
 ENOMEM

Three places panicked when a page or a page table could not be
allocated (redox-rpi#679):

- Grant::copy_mappings (fork, AddrSpaceWrapper::try_clone): a shared
  anonymous grant is mapped lazily, so the clone fills every untouched
  page, and expect("TODO: handle OOM") fired when memory ran out. Any
  process could reach it with a large MAP_SHARED mapping and fork().
  Now the grant is copied either completely or not at all: on an error
  the pages copied into the new address space are unmapped and their
  refs dropped, and fork fails with ENOMEM. A page table allocation
  failure in the new address space used to end the loop silently and
  left the child's grant half mapped (the rest would fault in as zero
  pages); it is now ENOMEM as well.
- Grant::transfer (moving grants, e.g. mremap): expect("TODO: OOM").
  It now maps all pages at the destination first, while they are still
  mapped at the source, and unmaps the source only when that worked. On
  failure the grant goes back unchanged and the move fails with ENOMEM.
- Grant::zeroed_phys_contiguous (DMA buffers through
  /scheme/memory/zeroed?phys_contiguous): expect("TODO: page table OOM").
  It now unmaps what it mapped, frees the range and returns ENOMEM.

No OOM kill: the call fails with ENOMEM.

Known limits: shared pages that copy_mappings filled in the parent
before the error stay in the parent (they are valid pages of its
mapping). A move of several grants in one call can stop after some of
them moved. Lazy sharing across fork, which would avoid filling the
untouched pages at all, needs back references and is a design change
upstream.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
